I reproduced the divergence directly (no command substitution, explicit `timeout 10`, byte-level `od -c` on stderr, three repeated runs, and from a different cwd — /root — to rule out the test14.py-readable traceback artifact). All five of the fuzzer's cases fail, and the normal cases still PASS, so the harness is not at fault.

The difference is substantive, not the accepted prog-name deviation: the reference emits `error: argument --c: expected one argument` while the port emits `error: unrecognized arguments: ...`. It is also not accepted deviations 2, 3 or 4.

Root cause in /output/lib/argparse/parser.mjs: `classify()` labels a bare `--` as `{ kind: 'positional', token: '--' }`, and the value-consumption branch in `parseArgs()` accepts any following entry with `kind === 'positional'`:
